winex11: Do not translate an unset message time when activating a window.

A new GUI thread can activate an unmanaged window before retrieving any
messages. Its message_time is zero. If another thread has already initialized
the process-wide X/Win32 clock adjustment, translating that zero yields a
stale timestamp. XSetInputFocus then silently leaves focus on the previous
window. In Force Commander, closing the splash reverts focus to the desktop,
preventing cursor clipping and the independent mouse-input grab from starting.

Use the existing CurrentTime fallback when no message time is available.
Keep timestamp conversion for real message times and leave X11's rejection
of stale focus requests intact.

The x11-initial-focus probe reproduces this on native Wine with Xvfb and
unmanaged windows, without forcing focus from the test harness.

diff --git a/dlls/winex11.drv/event.c b/dlls/winex11.drv/event.c
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/event.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/event.c
@@ -614,4 +614,6 @@ static void set_input_focus( struct x11drv_win_data *data )
-    if (EVENT_x11_time_to_win32_time(0))
+    /* A new GUI thread may not have retrieved a message yet. Zero is not
+     * an event timestamp; translating it can produce a stale X timestamp. */
+    if (NtUserGetThreadInfo()->message_time && EVENT_x11_time_to_win32_time(0))
         /* ICCCM says don't use CurrentTime, so try to use last message time if possible */
         /* FIXME: this is not entirely correct */
         timestamp = NtUserGetThreadInfo()->message_time - EVENT_x11_time_to_win32_time(0);
